#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "7fcfa422-4cc0-5798-8bc3-4824de1a3826" :question/math-academy-id "e-13266" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z < -1.54).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.063 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0618 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0606 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0749 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0735 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z <             -1.54) = P(Z \\leq -1.54) = \\Phi(-1.54).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/32adba8c3a4324eccce9b4ccbdb32ab2c2c3c76c3ad8216568b58f8bec0c8c46.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $\\Phi(-1.54)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $-1.5$ and the column that corresponds to $0.04.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.063 \\,$ | $\\color{blue}\\, 0.0618 \\,$ | $\\,0.0606\\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0749 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0735 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0918 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq  -1.54) = \\Phi(-1.54) = 0.0618\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059350 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/id #uuid "a376a597-d6ab-5d51-8c12-196ed793150d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13267" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z \\geq -1.16).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -1.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0901 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0885 \\,$ | $\\, 0.0869 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1075 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1056 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1038 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1271 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1251 \\,$ | $\\, 0.1230 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z \\geq -1.16).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/cac32ab36217820b63574e011488721bf57652fa63b4cbb7e5029fb54ce26243.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.16) & =1-P(Z<-1.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/48adcb6bd3b0d12b9bc935b2414d615e0014d905b89a9f7d632306cd30f08580.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(-1.16) =  0.1230.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.16) & =1-Φ(-1.16) \\\\  & =1-0.1230 \\\\  & =0.8770.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059351 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "d95c5015-9dc4-50a5-85bc-50bab9bf0650" :question/math-academy-id "e-13268" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ compute $P(-0.48 <              Z <              1.18).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2843 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2776 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8577 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8599 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8621 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8790 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8830 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8980 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8997 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9015 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2843 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2776 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3192 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3156 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3121 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3557 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3520 \\,$ | $\\, 0.3483 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8577 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8599 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8621 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8790 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8810 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8830 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8980 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8997 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9015 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.48 <     Z <   1.18).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/f9011a3355b8261e153842a43be45e9bb07240d88421e37f7297a7d0f807e101.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z \\leq z) = \\Phi(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.48<Z<1.18) & =P(Z<1.18)-P(Z\\le -0.48) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.18)-P(Z\\le -0.48) \\\\  & =Φ(1.18)-Φ(-0.48)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.48) & =0.3156 \\\\ Φ(1.18) & =0.8810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.48<Z<1.18) & =Φ(1.18)-Φ(-0.48) \\\\  & =0.8810-0.3156 \\\\  & =0.5654.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195c9f85-6e1e-5805-acbb-fb5a958c12ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361001} {:answer/id #uuid "d19bb30b-20c1-5788-9f95-c71dacc37b78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9871" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361002} {:answer/id #uuid "758dbea6-5f1a-59f4-bbbf-3c3f15abfcda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9826" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361003} {:answer/id #uuid "ae998bb4-b165-58c9-aaad-47f1597c514e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361004} {:answer/id #uuid "137f958b-730c-54ec-8fc6-dc6805337d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9783" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195c9f85-6e1e-5805-acbb-fb5a958c12ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9778" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361001} :answer-field/id #uuid "989efee3-32c0-57ed-a072-1f0977911d85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7740c74-dfbe-5d4f-ab8f-21b2b173a7a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131387" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<2.01).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z∼N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<2.01)=P(Z\\le 2.01)=Φ(2.01).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/300726cc3cff6cb943d627693bdea3da57ca7bb814988b55e9e3885a03faef42.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $Φ(2.01)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $2.0$ and the column that corresponds to $0.01.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ | $\\,0.9871\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le 2.01)=Φ(2.01)=0.9778.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4eeddcf-9856-55d2-abdb-1c0fab0624af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9292" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361008} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4b3bd4-be06-5e91-b8c4-e725cb751b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9177" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361009} {:answer/id #uuid "fa10320d-5bb3-563a-aaa0-2d9e2233b40a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9162" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361010} {:answer/id #uuid "5105eaa3-cf7a-517a-a8b2-0980a263b332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361011} {:answer/id #uuid "f08fbbf1-632b-517e-b4f9-1db43991e643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9306" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f08fbbf1-632b-517e-b4f9-1db43991e643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9306" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361012} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd4e9a24-db21-5d0e-b6f5-53242cf7d6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3138c83-a856-59a7-8ea4-c2fe34ba0a85" :question/math-academy-id "q-131390" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<1.48).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "If $Z∼N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<1.48)=P(Z\\le 1.48)=Φ(1.48).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/83df9b5a21d25104979949ceb25bffa7fca1ded99b671ca3c530b9cb94d4194a.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $Φ(1.48)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $1.4$ and the column that corresponds to $0.08.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le 1.48)=Φ(1.48)=0.9306.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d404e8c3-a117-58f9-8304-6b31c1160932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3936" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361015} {:answer/id #uuid "971a7f81-dabd-5472-b25f-44a7ecfbe55f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361016} {:answer/id #uuid "685d70cf-60e6-5131-ab71-26010f572eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4721" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361017} {:answer/id #uuid "93d409c6-4412-520c-aff1-5e7d8f579f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361018} {:answer/id #uuid "141a6268-9a0c-53ca-b48b-825882139557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141a6268-9a0c-53ca-b48b-825882139557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4325" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4465c58c-6504-54a4-8b76-1d55370e6128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "449f656d-2e72-51b8-aaf5-0f5765ff88e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-131397" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge 0.17).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5199\\,$ | $\\,0.5239\\,$ | $\\,0.5279\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ | $\\,0.5636\\,$ | $\\,0.5675\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge 0.17).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/759e123d5c61321e78b085260a14d5ae98a54b724b82b06cdecc46ff28072b8b.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.17) & =1-P(Z<0.17) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/83ab5f91c5ee81eeb4c31b42558af5c46cb59d74603b30913ab80dadeafc3317.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.17)=0.5675.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.17) & =1-Φ(0.17) \\\\  & =1-0.5675 \\\\  & =0.4325.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "648e4b9e-ce2e-526a-9cdb-089561728e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361022} {:answer/id #uuid "6950bf58-ff96-5727-81f0-993237d4c1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9793" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361023} {:answer/id #uuid "aefdf3e5-4a86-5cfc-9253-cc8939d2b7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9744" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361024} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fda556-0183-520a-ba28-03d7df527dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9803" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361025} {:answer/id #uuid "80b84037-932a-5262-a8d9-23a9286409fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9798" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "648e4b9e-ce2e-526a-9cdb-089561728e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9842" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361022} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d33e70-ea85-5372-8f97-49591ef34fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb82f793-122f-5348-98a6-3ce75647d47e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131399" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge -2.15).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0162\\,$ | $\\,0.0158\\,$ | $\\,0.0154\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0207\\,$ | $\\,0.0202\\,$ | $\\,0.0197\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge -2.15).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/f814b532742940206d119c1aa30746b128ab6ede249128e55fc949a1051220c9.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.15) & =1-P(Z<-2.15) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/9e2cf363bd0732c4669c4653d83f505826af605f2bc719a4f39135d233456b57.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-2.15)=0.0158.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -2.15) & =1-Φ(-2.15) \\\\  & =1-0.0158 \\\\  & =0.9842.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e376bffa-02ae-5575-af2c-4ad5e703f9fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "094edfb4-c2af-5b85-b61d-36f39f69ef4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361029} {:answer/id #uuid "370c1faa-52fa-5109-bd8b-1528ce67f08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9099" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c389f2-04cc-5187-a647-c5fd38a38195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9236" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361031} {:answer/id #uuid "04b0c65f-ec8b-5460-8228-e55c8997b437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9082" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361032} {:answer/id #uuid "33f190b2-4be5-5d1a-9f64-24476391a88d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9222" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "094edfb4-c2af-5b85-b61d-36f39f69ef4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361029} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94ff969-32d1-559d-8f36-0b823ed64a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4686a03b-30d0-5ee2-a537-502200e2f823" :question/math-academy-id "q-131400" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge -1.32).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge -1.32).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/d89bd5f38fdf4c952146dde8cd01207167c92cab07290742d229ceb0f4cf5712.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.32) & =1-P(Z<-1.32) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-1.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/0959a3c48bf70d10f24d85cd98bcd0703db4765ba5349d02896e18104c3f98a7.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.32)=0.0934.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge -1.32) & =1-Φ(-1.32) \\\\  & =1-0.0934 \\\\  & =0.9066.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba796ee2-cab1-544e-b94d-8278e26e5944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4154" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361036} {:answer/id #uuid "28741035-d43d-5d04-99fc-e92bba6e20b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4063" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361037} {:answer/id #uuid "21666eb2-5b31-55b9-8525-8e5a1ced01c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3472" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361038} {:answer/id #uuid "f247de43-2d95-5fbf-98f6-be4fca3b812c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3934" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361039} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8588bb-3d4b-58e6-927b-b75ae0ba4685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3690" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f247de43-2d95-5fbf-98f6-be4fca3b812c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3934" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361039} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3274f43-7f17-5600-973c-f40cbfc606c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca254cc-beaa-5bb8-b12c-1d05feed98c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-131518" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(0.1<Z<1.5).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.5040\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.5040\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9332\\,$ | $\\,0.9345\\,$ | $\\,0.9357\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9452\\,$ | $\\,0.9463\\,$ | $\\,0.9474\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(0.1<Z<1.5).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/806631e940119accaf9fa2dd9e52e59f4ae16480f237c2bb67ddc7796e4967c0.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.1<Z<1.5) & =P(Z<1.5)-P(Z\\le 0.1) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.5)-P(Z\\le 0.1) \\\\  & =Φ(1.5)-Φ(0.1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.1) & =0.5398 \\\\ Φ(1.5) & =0.9332\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.1<Z<1.5) & =Φ(1.5)-Φ(0.1) \\\\  & =0.9332-0.5398 \\\\  & =0.3934.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9134dbc8-b244-5976-8a4b-3c967db27cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1845" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361043} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5ea166-7526-56b8-af2d-57be2077b34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1378" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361044} {:answer/id #uuid "722a851f-f721-58c7-8348-1b8b569475a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361045} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb136aa-83aa-54c1-a52b-e142dbdce951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361046} {:answer/id #uuid "83435245-e8b2-5a20-865c-e7f25d821623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1770" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5ea166-7526-56b8-af2d-57be2077b34d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1378" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361044} :answer-field/id #uuid "b46d3290-3403-5448-806d-6e5d91cf42d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10f15d1-4ab0-5e7f-9001-186acf1e8688" :question/math-academy-id "q-131521" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-1.99<Z<-0.99).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0192\\,$ | $\\,0.0188\\,$ | $\\,0.0183\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0244\\,$ | $\\,0.0239\\,$ | $\\,0.0233\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0307\\,$ | $\\,0.0301\\,$ | $\\,0.0294\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0192\\,$ | $\\,0.0188\\,$ | $\\,0.0183\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0244\\,$ | $\\,0.0239\\,$ | $\\,0.0233\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0307\\,$ | $\\,0.0301\\,$ | $\\,0.0294\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-1.99<Z<-0.99).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/8dc66c8ceed4366688dc040104eaece3d251ecb9f72d9aae0827ead3fe933570.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.99<Z<-0.99) & =P(Z<-0.99)-P(Z\\le -1.99) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.99)-P(Z\\le -1.99) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.99)-Φ(-1.99).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.99) & =0.0233 \\\\ Φ(-0.99) & =0.1611\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.99<Z<-0.99) & =Φ(-0.99)-Φ(-1.99) \\\\  & =0.1611-0.0233 \\\\  & =0.1378.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dbb1a1f-b567-5677-9ae4-fb86cb3d574b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dbb1a1f-b567-5677-9ae4-fb86cb3d574b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3632" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361050} :answer-field/id #uuid "97452a97-7988-5aaa-855c-2766e8746cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11628d87-4dce-54ef-b67f-387a00fe09a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-330870" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<-0.35).$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ | $\\,0.3974\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ |\n\n$P(Z<-0.35)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $Z∼N(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<-0.35)=P(Z\\le -0.35)=Φ(-0.35).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/ab5be5e655c8daad36f35bee1abf3c82e3766b734da928be3dd060db551fbbb9.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $Φ(-0.35)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $-0.3$ and the column that corresponds to $0.05.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ | $\\,0.3192\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ | $\\,0.3557\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ | $\\,0.3974\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le -0.35)=Φ(-0.35)=0.3632.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "607e0800-870c-5271-9220-0489d9c5b891" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f20f107-94a7-5753-b465-69e642e03804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f20f107-94a7-5753-b465-69e642e03804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2498" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361053} :answer-field/id #uuid "386b646a-bd53-5754-809b-5f9a38e47d29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11265fa8-1f94-59fd-bbab-fa021bc7680e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334484" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-0.92<Z<-0.18).$ Use the tables below and do not round your answer.\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ | $\\,0.3897\\,$ | $\\,0.3859\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4325\\,$ | $\\,0.4286\\,$ | $\\,0.4247\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4721\\,$ | $\\,0.4681\\,$ | $\\,0.4641\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.3936\\,$ | $\\,0.3897\\,$ | $\\,0.3859\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4325\\,$ | $\\,0.4286\\,$ | $\\,0.4247\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4721\\,$ | $\\,0.4681\\,$ | $\\,0.4641\\,$ |\n\n$P(-0.92<Z<-0.18)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-0.92<Z<-0.18).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/34132169270b3259fb1be909f7aea2474e4dff1375603e9b9a75e013900f6340.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.92<Z<-0.18) & =P(Z<-0.18)-P(Z\\le -0.92) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.18)-P(Z\\le -0.92) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.18)-Φ(-0.92).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.92) & =0.1788 \\\\ Φ(-0.18) & =0.4286\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.92<Z<-0.18) & =Φ(-0.18)-Φ(-0.92) \\\\  & =0.4286-0.1788 \\\\  & =0.2498.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77015ca5-d392-500e-93dc-8e065dd1c06c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability on an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a3ad98-4ab3-556a-863a-86fb92be78e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7054" :db/id #edb/ref 175